In article <3778D7C4.5584DF66@star.zko.dec.com>, Joshua Cope <cope@star.zko.dec.com> writes:
>
>"Galaxy" has a specific meaning: it describes a "soft partitioned"
>environment where the hardware resources are allocated cooperatively
                                                        ^^^^^^^^^^^^^
>by the participating instances, not by the hardware. Contrast this with
>"hard partitioning," which is hardware and/or firmware-enforced (Sun's
>ES10000 system, for example), or partitioning via a monitor like IBM's 
>VM/ESA which provides a virtual machine to the operating system.

My experience with "cooperative" systems has been that when one player
doesn't cooperate, all is lost.  For instance, it used to that on a Mac if
one program refused to give up control that was it - none of the others
could sneak in.  That's one reason why time slice multitasking works so
well, the process in charge of slicing up time can overrule everybody else.
Whereas "cooperative" multitasking tends to hang.

What does galaxy do if one OS says "give me all the disks" or "give me all
the CPUs", or even "No, you may not have that extra CPU back!" ?  Who
has the final say?

Regards,

David Mathog
