Duncan Brown wrote:
> 
> Larry Kilgallen wrote:
> > Anticipating growth rate is a business judgement, having nothing to do
> > with the technology.  When growth rates go exponential, applications
> > stop scaling.  Writing every application so it can scale without limit
> > is overkill, especially when your business manager tell you to expect
> > moderate growth.
> 
> Hmmm, we've circled back into the point I keep seeing JF raise: nobody
> starts out with small VMS systems, which by virtue of things like SMP
> and clustering *can* scale to enormous sizes fairly easily.  So by
> losing the entire low end, VMS loses the foot in the door for most of
> the systems that grow to be in the high end.  (Not that I have a
> solution to that issue, but I do know from experience that VMS can be a
> good part of a strategy to handling an application that might grow at
> astonishing rates).
> 
> I still think someone who's *really* on the ball would choose their
> technology to minimize the pain of finding themselves on a rocketing
> growth curve.  But maybe that's just me!
> 
> Duncan

A couple of points here.  First, JF is definitely right, and when DEC
was doing well with the VAX they were too fat, dumb, and happy to realize
that they should have remained lean and hungary least they be gobbled up
by someone who was.  Ain't hindsight 20-20?

As far as a new and growing company, issues such as "Will we even be here
tomorrow?" will get much more attention than "How're we going to rule the
world?"  For many new ventures, maybe most, the first issue is their fate.
Of those that will make it, very few will have thought out the growth issues
before it becomes a problem, and to be fair, most will not have to handle
growth as explosive as EBAY's.  Most entrepreneurs do not think about
technical details, and most technical people who believe in structure and
planning do not become entrepreneurs.  Before I'm flamed, notice I said most,
not all.

