Jeffrey Keith Boulier wrote:
> 
> In article <37792C2D.5F68498A@videotron.ca>,
> JF Mezei  <jfmezei.spamnot@videotron.ca> wrote:
> >What about IBM's current partitionning scheme (dates back from early 90s on
> >390s) which does NOT involve VM ? How does that compare to Galaxies (without
> >VMS's galaxy additions ?)

There is no S/390 scheme that does not involve a hypervisor.
There is a *cluster* organization called Parallel Sysplex.
Partitions can certainly be members of a Sysplex, just like any
other independent OS image.

> >
> >(Sorry, I forgot the name of that IBM technology that allows multiple
> >instances to run on same hardware without VM running).

Doesn't exist.

> 
> That's an LPAR. I believe it is more like Sun's E10000 scheme, 

No, LPAR = Logical PARtition = hypervisor-based partitioning.
Sun's dynamic domains are effectively PPAR (Physical PARtition).

> except that
> IBM's method is atomic to the extent that one can dedicate percentages of
> a single CPU between partitions. 

Yes, and that's done by the scheduler in the hypervisor.
Effectively time-sharing at a higher (or lower?) level.

> Your OS/2 workstation [the master
> console; it has some funny name] functions somewhat like the E10000's SSP
> in its ability to shift resources between the system images.

It's the S/390 HSC (Hardware System Console, I think --
definitely HSC). You make it sound like it has some of the
facilities of the Sun SSP; it has lots more.

There are people in IBM who believe Sun named its E10000 console
the SSP in order to deliberately confuse customers into thinking
it has the capabilities of the RS/6000 SP PSSP code, which it
does not. Paranoid? Possibly. But it's pretty clear that they
named their resource manager SRM after the S/390 SRM, which has
about, oh, 100X the function. Or naming the Sun Storage
Architecture = SSA = a disk technology they in fact do not
support. Sounds like marketing Judo to me. Makes me think of
various slimy ... nevermind, I'm not a marketeer. Maybe that's
why.

> I'm pretty sure that one LPAR can't dynamically request more resources for
> itself - the request must come from the master console.

I believe it's true that an LPAR can't directly request
resources. 

Greg Pfister
<not my employers' opinion>



