>> Just in case the religion-of-the-week answer (or any other one involving
>> a full server at each end) isn't the one you're looking for, there are
>> modem-based routers around.
>
>Yeah, all these "better" ways will cost more than Linux PCs, and when he's
>done using the bridge, he'll have expensive gear unadaptable to any other
>purpose.

You seem to be deliberately missing the point.

Linux PCs *won't work* for this application.  They can't actually
*bridge* raw Ethernet frames over PPP.  If he needed a router, that would
be fine, but LAT doesn't work that way -- you have to bridge it, sending
an exact copy of every Ethernet frame intended for the other end over
the PPP link.  Linux doesn't support a PPP encapslation that can do that.

Plenty of dedicated routers won't work, either.  He needs a bridge.

