:>That depends on your definition of "big iron".  I understand at DECUS
:>OpenVMS demonstated running Linux as an instance in a Galaxy, with was
:>either a AlphaServer 4100 or 8400.

  The DECUS technology demonstration was on an AlphaServer 4100.

  I would expect to see Linux on the AlphaServer 8400, on the AlphaServer
  GS60, and on the AlphaServer GS140 (if it is not there already), as well 
  as other future boxes -- please note that Linux qualification and support 
  work is not occuring in OpenVMS Engineering, but we are obviously working 
  with Linux...

:So they ported Linux to the Galaxy "virtual hardware"?

  OpenVMS Galaxy is not a virtual machine.  Linux and OpenVMS were
  running on the AlphaServer 4100 box, using on the OpenVMS Galaxy 
  firmware -- the firmware presents each instance with the appearance
  of a specific subset of the hardware present in the entire box (as 
  configured by the system manager).

  That Linux was operating with an OpenVMS Galaxy configuration and
  swapping software was a result of some changes to Linux to support
  processor migration, and that Linux runs on Alpha.

:  That's kinda cool,
:I guess.  Gee, considering we run on almost every box Digital ever built,
:it'd be nice if we could get that kind of engineering assistance.  Or
:ever could have...

  I'll let the author speak to the effort involved in getting Linux
  to operate in an OpenVMS Galaxy and to migrate processors, but we 
  were able to demonstrate Windows NT operating in the same box as 
  OpenVMS (and Unix) several years ago, and with no changes to the 
  Windows NT operating system.  (We are much more able to make changes 
  and enhancements to Linux than to Windows NT, for obvious reasons.)

:Does Linux run on the actual 4100 or 8400 hardware yet?  Again, if so, good
:for them -- we have for years.

  OpenVMS Galaxy is not a virtual machine.

  Linux was running on the actual AlphaServer 4100 hardware.

