JF Mezei wrote:

>Is there such a thing a "Galaxy" which is the firmware which has allowed
>multiple instances of operating systems to exist on the same box with 
>physical resources being allocated to each by the Galaxy management 
>software ?

"Galaxy" has a specific meaning: it describes a "soft partitioned"
environment where the hardware resources are allocated cooperatively
by the participating instances, not by the hardware. Contrast this with
"hard partitioning," which is hardware and/or firmware-enforced (Sun's
ES10000 system, for example), or partitioning via a monitor like IBM's 
VM/ESA which provides a virtual machine to the operating system.

In Galaxy, there is only minimal firmware support - just enough for the
instances to negotiate ownership of the hardware among themselves. Each 
instance sees and interacts with the hardware directly, just as on a 
nonpartitioned machine. 

Ed Wilts wrote:
> 
> ...  The work demonstrated on the Linux side was simply moving
> the CPU back and forth; at no time did I see both Linux cpus activelly
> processing while one cpu was being yanked.

Correct; we modified Linux just enough to notice an incoming CPU, and
to be able to migrate it back over to the OpenVMS instance. We didn't 
add the equivalents to "START/CPU" and "STOP/CPU" to Linux.

