Bob Hassinger wrote:
> In addition, perhaps it would be prudent in a case like this, were it
> should be easy to anticipate high growth rates, to start by selecting
> designs and technologies that will degrade gracefully under heavy load
> rather than precipitously collapsing.

Ahhh hindsight is always so much better.

When canadian banks implemented Interac-Debit (point of sale debit card
system) back in the early 90s, they were paranoid about performance and what
type of growth and customer acceptance would occur. They first rolled out in
one city (Ottawa). Then they rolled out to Quebec and British Columbia. And
finally to the rest of Canada.  But it was not without hiccups. I still
remember christmas eve in a food supermarket with everyone buying with
interact-debit but the system was overloaded and could not process
transactions (what do you do, all your food has been packed in bags, but you
can't pay ?, and all cash registers are stuck that way ?).

Needless to say that it gave banks metrics to work with so that when they
rolled out nationwide, they had a good idea of what to expect for the big
rushes (mothers day, easter and christmas).


The problem with internet web sites is that it is very hard to roll out
"slowly". The minute you are on-line, you are on-line to a whole country,
unless you really do restrict registrations to folks of certain states.

So it ios much harder to plan for growth. Architecture or not, if you have no
idea if you will grow 15% or 300% in a year, it is not an easy task.

But I agree, that a VMS cluster allowing up to 96 nodes is perhaps the best
and most elegant (and least disruptive) way to increase capacity once you are
setup properly with the foundations and design.

On the other hand, many systems start off as a pilot to test the waters, and
once started, there is no room to stop and think because you are so busy
fighting the fires caused by the rapid growth.



