Larry Kilgallen wrote:
> Anticipating growth rate is a business judgement, having nothing to do
> with the technology.  When growth rates go exponential, applications
> stop scaling.


Probolem is that without any precedent, managers have no way or tools to
predict what sort of growth rates are possible and even less proeduct what is
likely. In a year or two, there will have been enough precedents and the hype
over "dot com" will have died down a bit and groth will be more realistic.

Remember DEC that was claiming it was the fastest growing PC maker at one time
? (because they went from selling 0 to 10 PC,s they growth rate was infonite
and thus higher than anyone else).

But when you grow from 10 to 15, it becomes a lot easier to manage.

Also, a lot of capacity planning was done with "transactions per second" from
banking or other transactional applications. Planning for "lengthy and ill
defined" transactions is very hard.

Doing a transaction in an ATM results ina  very predictable load on the CPU
and telecom infrastructure. (fixed size transactions). But on the WEB, how do
you measure in advance what your customers's clicking habits will be like ?
You have to constantly monitor loads and hopefully adapt very quickly.



