A KA660 is a MicroVAX 4000-200 CPU card, 5VUPs, not a speed demon but
much better than a uVAX II.  It has an onboard DSSI and ethernet
controller.  It was a common field upgrade for the MV3300/3400 KA640 CPU
card.  IIRC it accepts up to 64MB of memory (or was it 128?).  The
CQD-220 cards are the perhaps the best SCSI controllers ever made for
the Q-BUS, you lucked out on those, they work with just about any SCSI
tape or disk drive.  They are MSCP devices, they look like KDA or RQDX
diskcontrollers and TK tape controllers to VMS.  The DELQA is an
ethernet controller, my guess is this machine had 2 ethernet segments
(later versions of VMS supported this).

Not sure how well this machine would work for Linux but it would make a
nice VMS machine.  The CMD controllers even support dual-host SCSI in
VMS if you wanted to set up a VAX Cluster.  I have a nearly identical
machine in the office, used for an array of 8mm tape drives to back up a
cluster.  It's being retired shortly, I hope to take it home.
       Jack Peacock






