In article <3771A1CF.3407D876@videotron.ca>, JF Mezei <jfmezei.spamnot@videotron.ca> writes:
>Bob Hassinger wrote:
>> I find it interesting that what seems to be a high visibility 7 by 24 by
>> forever type of operation is configured in a way that requires announced
>> down time like this seems to be.  I am under the impression it is quite
>> feasible to do this sort of thing so the users never see an impact 
>
>Some applications allow you to store transactions until the back-end is back
>up for "delayed" processing. ATMs often work that way with a Tandem acting as
>the front-end if the IBM mainframe is unavailable and the Tandem accumulating
>trasactions (blind authorisations against just a minimum of checks (PIN,
>negative file) and sending them to the IBM once it becomes available again.
>
>This is usually transparent to the user because these downtimes are at night
>when branches are closed. There were interesting "application" if you had
>knowledge of such downtimes though...
>
>But for other applications, such a structure may not be possible because
>execution MUST be executed right away otherwise the customer sees the
>situation as "downtime".
>
>If bids have  hard deadline and ebay canjot process all bids before that
>deadline because many are backlogged due to downtime, then the legal bid you
>made will not make it to the seller before the deadline even though you made
>it before.


You can of corse overcome this type of problem with clustering.

A VMS cluster can be configured with a cluster alias. All connections goto the 
cluster alias and then are directed to the least loaded system in the cluster.
When you want to do work on one node in the cluster you simply disable logins
to that node so that all future connections goto the other cluster node(s).
Then wait a little while to make sure all current connections on the node have
completed (If these are users actually logged in to the system eg Telnet
connections you may have to wait sometime or tell the users to logout and login
again).
When noone is using the system you can then take it down and do your work with
no impact on the users. With the appropriate hardware (eg shadowed system disk)
you can even do a rolling upgrade of the operating system with virtually no
downtime to the users. (There may be a small amount of disruption due to the
fact that to complete the upgrade each node will in turn need to be rebooted
but as far as the users are concerned they will at most be asked to logout and
login a couple of times).

As far as transaction processing is concerned there is no storing of
transactions since the system (in the form of the cluster ) is always 
available.
Of course according to what hardware you have and when you do this work the
effect maybe that processing of transactions is slowed down - after all in
taking down a system from the cluster you are reducing the clusters capacity. 



David Webb
VMS and Unix team leader
CCSS
Middlesex University



