In article <37720DBB.7C2BA141@gtech.com>, Arne =?iso-8859-1?Q?Vajh=F8j?= <avajhoej@gtech.com> writes:
>"D.Webb" wrote:
>> You can of corse overcome this type of problem with clustering.
>> 
>> A VMS cluster can be configured with a cluster alias. All connections goto the
>> cluster alias and then are directed to the least loaded system in the cluster.
>
>Assuming that the software can be used cluster-wide. VMS delivers
>facilities
>like cluster-wide locking, but not all software uses them.
>
>Arne

True,

Some software such as databases do their own thing bypassing RMS and
other system facilities. They may also use process synchronisation features
and memory features provided by VMS such as global sections which are fine 
for single nodes but do not work across nodes. For these applications you 
would need the cluster aware versions of the software such as Oracle's 
parallel server.

I would hazard though that the vast majority of applications running on Unix or
NT systems could be ported to VMS with little or no regard to clustering and 
still end up being able to be run on multiple nodes in a cluster.


David Webb
VMS and Unix team leader
