From: frisbie@flying-disk.spamblock.com (Alan Frisbie)
Subject: Ethernet link between two buildings
Date: 1999/06/24
Message-ID: <1999Jun24.101826.1586@flying-disk.spamblock.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 493454220
Organization: Flying Disk Systems, Inc.
Newsgroups: comp.dcom.telecom.tech,comp.os.vms,comp.sys.dec

I need to link two ethernet (10 Mbps) segments between two
buildings that are about 0.5 mile apart.   The details are:

  1) The buildings are *almost* line of sight.   The problem
     is a freeway overpass that just barely blocks the sight
     line between the two rooftops (even considering a 10'
     mast at each end).

  2) This only needs to operate for about four weeks, while
     everything is moved from one building to the other.
     Thus, cost is an important consideration.

  3) Both buildings are served by the same phone central office.

  4) The ethernet traffic is all DEC LAT (terminal servers) and
     DECnet, with no TCP/IP.

Things we are considering:

  1) Wireless (900 MHz or 2.4 GHz) Ethernet bridge.   Black Box
     has one called the BestLAN Bridge for around $2700/end.
     The 2.4 GHz unit offers 2 Mbps speed, while the 900 MHz one
     is only 860 Kbps.   The issue here is the line of sight.
     Do these need a pure line-of-sight, or do they provide some
     degree of refraction over/around obstacles?   Who else
     makes units like this?

  2) Some kind of connection (T1 or ???) from the telephone company.
     What types of connections are typically available (and at
     what price), and what equipment would be required at each
     end.

  3) A full-blown, professionally-installed rental microwave link.
     Who does this sort of thing?   How much might it cost?

  4) (Not entirely serious) A surreptittious fiber optic link
     along the adjacent railroad tracks and freeway bridge.   This
     would have to be about 3/4 mile long.   What kind of fiber
     and end equipment would be required, and what would it cost?

     (This idea came up because two big telephone companies run
     their cross-country fiber lines right along the same railroad
     tracks.)   We might even be able to get permission to do this,
     but the bureaucratic hassles (a railroad *and* the state
     highway department) would only be worth it if this were by
     far the cheapest method.

What other solutions should we be looking at?   Is this sort of
short-term link available for rental?   From whom?

-- 
