Alan Frisbie <frisbie@flying-disk.spamblock.com> writes:

>   1) Wireless (900 MHz or 2.4 GHz) Ethernet bridge.   Black Box
>      has one called the BestLAN Bridge for around $2700/end.
>      The 2.4 GHz unit offers 2 Mbps speed, while the 900 MHz one
>      is only 860 Kbps.   The issue here is the line of sight.
>      Do these need a pure line-of-sight, or do they provide some
>      degree of refraction over/around obstacles?

Technically you're supposed to have clearance out to the first Fresnel zone
(see http://www-library.itsi.disa.mil/org/fed_std/html/dir-016/_2398.htm for
a definition of that) although in this case you may likely get by as the
path isn't very long (2600 feet) - if you can get something to try and make
some signal strength measurements that'd be the best way to pin down what
will work here.  You may see some knife-edge refraction off the overpass
and that would help.  Or there may be some large object nearby that could
be used as a passive reflector...

I have a C-band satellite dish that's partially blocked by a brick fence
and I still get usable signal levels, but they are down about 30-40% from
where they would be without the fence in the way.

>      Who else
>      makes units like this?

Among others there are http://www.breezecom.com and http://www.ezylink.com

>   3) A full-blown, professionally-installed rental microwave link.
>      Who does this sort of thing?   How much might it cost?

The phone company can also do temporary microwave links - I've used
these for video transmission, they may be able to put data on one.
This has the advantage of being a licensed service with hopefully less
interference - I believe microwave ovens are close enough to 2.4GHz to
cause problems, for instance..

There must be some other vendors but I don't know who.  Once place to
look for them is http://www.millenniumtel.com/ref-wles.htm

