	tsmurphy@students.uiuc.edu (Terry Murphy) writes:

> I won't argue that Windows is doing more work than Solaris is, but
> serving up 1.5 billion mostly dynamic pages per month hardly seems 
> like a small task to me. And THAT part of eBay never crashes (AFAIK)

As someone who browsed Ebay as a cutesy novelty for a month or so
last year, I can attest that snappy and reliable web serving would
not be the most apt description of their IIS farm.

Those webpages also not "mostly dynamic" by nature, although I'm
sure IIS is wonderful at preventing you from mixing largely static
content with a few bits of dynamic stuff. I can't speak for how
Ebay is actually designed, but in principle there are only about
four strings that change on any particular top-level auction page.

NT is also the only system I know in which running something as
trivial as a webserver is actually a big reliability accomplishment.




