JF Mezei  <jfmezei.spamnot@videotron.ca> wrote:
%% "D.Webb" wrote:
%% > A VMS cluster can be configured with a cluster alias. All connections
%% > goto the  cluster alias and then are directed to the least loaded
%% > system in the cluster.
%% 
%% Fine and dandy. But how is the cluster-alias imlemented in terms of
%% availability ? Isn't there a single system which handles the
%% load-balancing to direct incoming transasctions to the least loaded
%% system ? Or is that implemented in a single router ?

  I can't speak to how VMS does it, but my cluster alias mechanism
simply relies on delegating a domain via DNS, and configuring a 
special purpose DNS server to resolve that domain (the domain in this
case is a domain of one -- the cluster alias).  With DNS, you can 
specify as many name servers as you want to resolve a domain.  So,
with my script mechanism, you can have several servers each running
the node checker/alias definer script.  If one of them is down, a dns
client will simply go on to the next one in the list.  No single point
of failure.  I would imagine VMS works better than some gunk I cobbled 
together in an afternoon.  Regardless, the way DNS works makes it
at least possible to avoid a single point of failure in resolving
the cluster alias.

%% 
%% Sounds like a possible single point of failure to me.
%% 
%% That is why, in the good old VAX days, Digital (remember them ?) had the
%% VAXFT. a fault tolerant small front end to direct transactions to an available
%% processing node.
%% 
%% Come to think of it, it might be wise for Compaq to pitch the following config:
%% 
%% A tandem fault tolerant front end acting as the router to direct transactions
%% to a VMS cluster.





