In article <1999Jun24.101826.1586@flying-disk.spamblock.com>, frisbie@flying-disk.spamblock.com (Alan Frisbie) writes:
>
>  1) The buildings are *almost* line of sight.   The problem
>     is a freeway overpass that just barely blocks the sight
>     line between the two rooftops (even considering a 10'
>     mast at each end).

Radio waves will diffract to some extent.

>  1) Wireless (900 MHz or 2.4 GHz) Ethernet bridge.   Black Box
>     has one called the BestLAN Bridge for around $2700/end.
>     The 2.4 GHz unit offers 2 Mbps speed, while the 900 MHz one
>     is only 860 Kbps.   The issue here is the line of sight.
>     Do these need a pure line-of-sight, or do they provide some
>     degree of refraction over/around obstacles?   Who else
>     makes units like this?

The 900 MHz will diffract somewhat more than the 2.4 GHz.
If your line of sight is really blocked, I think you have
a better chance with the 900 MHz unit.  Are any of the dealers
willing to bring a couple of units out and do a field test
before you buy?

From "Communications News", advertisements for people making
wireless links include Lucent Technologies www.wavelan.com
The ad specifically lists building to building.

Breezecom advertisies wireless network connections: the ad
says up to 8 miles outdoors.  www.breezecom.com

Glenayre Western Multiplex www.WirelessInterconnect.com
advertises license and license free systems, but they
start at 2.4 GHz and might need line of sight.

Someone else mentioned having to get FCC clearences, etc.  There
are now provisions for running certain types of wireless links
without having to get a site license (just as you don't have to
get a license to run a baby monitor).  Just to be sure, you should
ask the vendor.

If you can get permission to run the fiber (which might not be
too hard to get if it's temporary and not buried) you might
want to look at http://www.tccomm.com for fiber to Ethernet
media converters (I haven't worked with them, just picking this
up from an ad in "Communications News").  They say they will
drive up to 100 km of fiber.  Another vendor with an ad for
similar equipment is www.imcnetworks.com

You might want to try to get a copy of the June 1999 issue of
Communications News magazine, which has quite a few articles
on wireless LANs and other wireless communications.  If it's
not in your local library try www.comnews.com


