"Alan Winston - SSRL Admin Cmptg Mgr" <winston@SSRL.SLAC.STANFORD.EDU> writes:
> I got kind of carried away and bought an AlphaStation 200 4/233 and a 4/166
> on Ebay.  I'd like to use the OpenVMS Hobbyist License on them.  Both are 
> short of memory; one has 48mb, the other 24; the 7.2 SPD says you need 
> 64mb to boot and login.  Poking around the web I've found some memory that
> seems pretty expensive for systems I bought cheaply; does anybody have any
> suggestions for AlphaStation 200 memory sources?  Used is okay if it's
> guaranteed.  I'd like to get them each up to 96 mb if I can afford it.

  These take generic 60ns 36-bit fast page mode memory, with tin leads (they 
actually only need 33-bit memory, but the generic 36-bit stuff is cheaper).
I use Kingston memory exclusively (and this box takes parts like KTM8X36-60T)
but you can use any old SIMMs, as long as they're the type described above.
[Note: some folks have reported problems with some 32MB SIMMs, but I've never
met ones that don't work, as long as they're the correct type.]

  The maximum supported memory is 384MB (6 64MB SIMMs), though the box seems
quite happy at 768MB (6 128MB SIMMs). Put the larger (capacity, not physical
size) SIMMs in the lower bank numbers.

> While I'm at it, I'd like to upgrade the disks.  One 2GB, the other 1GB.  Is it
> readily known what variant of SCSI (fast, ultra, wide, fast wide, plain
> vanilla, etc) these systems support?  Can you recommend third party disks that
> will work in these systems?

  Fast narrow single-ended ("vanilla") SCSI. Current VMS releases seem happy
with generic disks; older ones tended to barf with "extended sense data recei-
ved" or "fatal drive error" with new-ish 3rd-party drives. Patch kits (of the
form ALPSCSI...) were available to fix some of these foibles.

