Larry Kilgallen wrote:
> Perhaps Art Rice could say something about the manner in which NSK
> fixes are regimented.


Tandem shops usually are very serious financial institutions which understand
that system stability is of upmost importance. In Canada, every month, there
is/was a report issued by the association which run the interbank point of
sales and ATM system (Interac) which shows each bank's uptime. And employees
at one bank for which I worked would either be "rewarded" or "punished" if
that bank gained or lost in its ranking against other banks.

As a result, the bank had very stringent procedures to implement and test ANY
changes to that (and most) environments. 

And no change to the operating system could be made without the consulting the
makers of the apps running on that machine.


It is possible that eBAY knew about available patches, but that the makers of
the sofware had not yet certified them for their apps so eBAY didn't install
them. It is also possible that eBAY was a "PC minded" company that didn't care
so much about system availability and robustness.


Having a rigid structure does improve system availability because it slows
down change. But in these days, slowing down change is also dangerous for the
company's survival, especially for an internet company.

When I was at that bank, I had enough knowledge of my cluster that I could
accurately predict the ikpact of a change and be able to test it overnight
before the next morning's big rush. And very often, I found myself battling
with authorities who insisted such a change should be planned weeks or a month
ahead (such as adding a terminal to a dec-server, which for the ibm mainframe
environment, required a system reboot to have that terminal activated, hence
their desired to have that type of planning).


The point is that if you know the system very well, it is easier to know the
impact. But when tasks are divided and nobody has the time to get a total
picture of the system, then you start to have problems because nobody can
predict the impact of a change. This is why in that bank, that had a commitee
for changes for each platform where each "stakeholder" department had a
representative  and they would then report back later on whether their staff
thought there would be any impact for a specific change.

(This was to prevent an update of CICS required by a mortgage application to
affect negatively the consumer  loan application as an example).

What I find interesting is that when I was there, I thought that their
structure was way too heavy, but now, I do see the value of it since I have
worked in those "PC" shops that have no clue on system stability and handling
of changes.



