I'm sure some others will elaborate on this.
If you subscribe to HOTSTUFF which is the e-mail method
that Tandem uses to notify users of IPMs, (Interim Product
Modifications) you will get timely notice of "undocumented features"
and the Fix to remove that feature.  There are also other methods of
receiving the notifications, I just happen to prefer the e-mail.

This last year there have been a ton of them, mostly concerning Y2K on
both the K and S series Himalayas, and Most of the rest concerned the
ever evolving G-series OS for the S-series Himalays. (server net
technology).  There have been a few for TCP/IP, Transaction Monitoring
Facility, etc. But not a large Number.

Tandem will, like all vendors, point to the IPM that was not installed
if the problem was related to a missing IPM.  I have never had to
install an IPM because most of them concern products that we do not
use, or features that we do not care to use at this time.  I will also
be installing the most recent OS in a few weeks, which includes all
Y2K fixes, so I will not waste my tme with all of the Y2K IPMs.

BTW, the NSK systems have really been Y2K Compliant almost for a long
time. All critical process take place using a 4 word timestamp.  It is
the ancilarry products and the "shell" known as TACL that needed the
Y2K fixes.

Even so, I will have Tandem make the trip out in July to Install the
D48 NSK, pay the extra and they are responsible if there are problems
related to this years very important update.  Normally I would do it
myself but upper management wants to take any possibility of blame and
put it on the vendors where Y2K is involved.

