John Johnstone <johnstone@tridentusa.nspam.com> writes:
> Does this recommendation apply to all systems or just the AlphaStation 200?  We've
> configured several types of Alpha workstation systems here with larger banks 
> after smaller ones but we haven't had any problems so far.  Are we just lucky? :-)

  In general, memory controllers fall into 2 groups: those that don't care,
and those that insist on biggest first. So using biggest first is always safe.

  Some systems with controllers that want biggest-first can work when other
layouts are used - but you wind up with "holes" in the physical address space.
A CPU implementation with double page tables (like all VAXes except the RTVAX,
and presumably the Alpha) can hide this from most of the operating system and
applications, though.

