<knbraam@worldnet.att.net> wrote:
>I got an old DEC computer from work and when I try to use it , it just boots
>to a blue screen asking for a password. Is there any way around this
>password? I would like to be able to reformat the HD so I can install
>windows and let my kids use it. It formerly had very specialized software on
>it, thats why I wanted to reformat and start from scratch. I figured it
>would just boot to DOS and it would be a snap from there. But it didn't work
>out that way. Any suggestions?

Most probably the password is implemented at the BIOS/boot
level.

The traditional fix is to open the computer, find the
battery that keeps the CMOS data, disconnect it,
(If it is soldered there is sometimes a jumper to disable
it) wait for a (very long) while, connect it back and
reboot..

The only problem with this approach is that, together with
the password,  you will loose all the configuration info,
including the disk drive geometry information.

Most modern motherboards can identify the disk
automatically, but this may not be the case with your
computer.

Some people recommend shortening the battery for a couple
of seconds to achieve the same..
Please don't do that ! In addition to the risk of damaging
the battery, it may not help at all since CMOS circuits
could go for hours on the charge left in filtering caps,
etc. I would rather desolder or cut the wires to the battery
and solder them again later.

RW.



