dunnett@mala.bc.ca (Malcolm Dunnett) writes:

>In article <376c57a6.615181894@news-server.tampabay.rr.com>, 
>   arice1@tampabay.rr.com (Art Rice) writes:

>> I thought that was IBM's philosophy.
>> 

>   It was, but in the early 80's Digital decided that the way to 
>success lay in emulating IBM. The rest, as they say, is history ...

At the risk of introducing subject skew in a USENET article, I think
the Microsoft monopoly case is premature. IBM's monopoly was immense.
I was at DEC from 1974-78 and recall one case where DEC and IBM bid on
a system.  IBM's system was about 3X as expensive as the DEC PDP-10 bid.
IBM got the business and the customer told DEC that it concluded DEC's bid
was so low the bidded system have been underpowered.

One of the key pieces in IBM's monopoly was unparalleled customer support
that Microsoft can't match.  You could buy IBM and know they'd be there
to help.  While you can't do that for one PC, Microsoft ought to be able
to do that for customers with thousands.

As for what happened to DEC & VMS, I was at Alliant Computer (out of business
in 1992, but we built some nice mini-supercomputers in our day) in the late
80s.  Early on, Alliant had trouble selling systems "Unix?  No way, all our
people are used to DCL, I wish you had a DCL shell!"

So Alliant contracted outside the company for a DCL shell.  It took a couple
rounds before it was acceptable.  So we tried that to help the sale.  "DCL?
We finally bit the bullet - there were too many good deals with Unix and
all our new hires know it, so we're a Unix shop now and gave the Vaxen to
payroll."

The new computers companies like Alliant readily adopted Unix, there was
no cheaper path to get a OS on a new architecture.  Other new companies
were forming that didn't have a VMS shop, and they liked what these Unix
upstarts had to offer.

After Alliant crashed, I returned to DEC, but my then it was too late to
see DEC realize the world was changing - it was already well changed.

