Things aren't really _that_ primitive in the UK. I'll e-mail Julian with
info on a couple of firms that may be able to help, but not put their
names here in case they can't.

Using a recovery service is likely to be expensive, so if you don't
think it's worth it, here are some suggestions for a DIY approach, but
be aware that any further use of the "dead" disk may reduce its
recoverability by a professional service.

If the dead disk still spins up, try doing a BACKUP/PHYSICAL from the
old disk to your empty new one, followed by ANALYZE/DISK/REPAIR on the
new disk. If this looks promising, work out what is still missing and
use the undelete utility (called FLORIAN) from the FREEWARE disk to get
back some not-so-old file versions. In desperation, and if you can
MOUNT the dead disk /FOREIGN, you could use VFE (also on the FREEWARE
disk) in device mode, searching the whole disk for strings you know are
in the lost file(s), assuming they're ASCII that is. This, however,
could be a loooong job (I know; I once had to do something similar back
in the days when disks were only a few Mb).

Chris


