Peter da Silva wrote:
> In article <7k7mjo$hpk$1@news.mel.aone.net.au>,
> Geoff Roberts <geoffrob@stmarks.pp.catholic.edu.au> wrote:
> >        o  Cisco MultiNet for OpenVMS, any version
> >        o  PathWay, Attachmate Inc., any version
> >        o  TCPware, Process Software Corporation, any version
> >        o  CMU TCP/IP  -NOT SUPPORTED-
> 
> Is this true, there's four separate incompatible TCP/IP APIs on VMS?

There are 5+ TCP/IP packages for VMS (UCX is missing from the list
above,
which BTW is seriously out-of-date, since Multinet is now also Process
and not Cisco and Attachmate has dumped PathWay).

I would not call it 5+ seperate incompatible API's. All (AFAIK)
support standard socket interface.

And therefore many internet program will build on all
without any #ifdef's.

But problems can arise:
  - small differences in some of the extensions (especially when
    you want to optimize performance)
  - different link libraries etc.
  - different command utilities

It is not unusal to see #ifdef's in VMS code to distinguish between
different TCP/IP packages.

I think you can compare porting an internet program from VMS with
Multinet
to VMS with UCX to porting the same program from Linux to Solaris. All
the
basic stuff is the same, but there are always some details that needs
tweaking.

Arne



