Steven et al,

Let me add to what I stated earlier.  Yesterday, I got several surplus
DECpc XL 466D2 systems, the kind with the 486s and an older main logic
board than the 54-23606-01 found in Celebris XL 5xxx systems.  I
dropped a Pentium processor card in place of the 486 processor card,
and the system was happy with it, and QAPLUS diagnostics found no
problems.  Only cosmetic issue is that when the CPU speed exceeds
100MHz, the Version 1.21 BIOS still reports just 100MHz.  This is
because the original BIOS was written by people who could think no
faster than 100MHz, the fastest Pentium available at the time.  With
the CPU card I tried, however, the CPU ran at its rated speed of 120
MHz.  I suspect that faster CPUs will run at rated speed, too, but I
have my doubts about dual-processor setups.

BTW, I intend to upgrade these systems to 133Mhz Pentium bare bones
setups and sell them.  By bare bones, I mean the CPU, main logic
board, CPU card, 256K external cache module, floppy diskette drive,
SCSI, IDE and floppy cables.  Latest V1.23 Y2K-compliant BIOS.  All
dust bunnies cleaned out, tested and warranteed for 90 days.  New
Celebris XL plastic front panel, while I have them.  If you're
interested, send me a reply... Ben Myers

