My feeling is that way too many companies are relying on NT-expert-wannabies
who grew up with instant computer expertise because they were good on their
Nintendos. And because managers can't judge expertise, they beleive the
expert-wannabe and hire him/her/it instead of buying into expertise.

The result is that systems are architectured with small systems in-mind
instead of being architectured with a large disaster tolerant mentality and
once they have started to operate, they don't have the time or resources to
change everything.

(This is why I strongly beleive it critical to have entry systems for VMS so
that companies can start small and then grow).


If a company doesn't have "serious systems" expertise, then it is normal to
expect that there will not be proper testing or software upgrade policies.



