Alan Winston - SSRL Admin Cmptg Mgr <winston@ssrl.slac.stanford.edu> wrote:
> 64mb to boot and login.  Poking around the web I've found some memory that
> seems pretty expensive for systems I bought cheaply; does anybody have any
> suggestions for AlphaStation 200 memory sources?  Used is okay if it's
> guaranteed.  I'd like to get them each up to 96 mb if I can afford it.

I've found used memory the way to go.  We've got a place locally called
"Computer Renisance", they test the memory, but since I won't let them
install it, they won't guarantee it (like I'll let PC types play with a
VMS box).  Just make sure that you can return the RAM if they sell you the
wrong type!  The first place I bought it from sold me two 32Mb sticks, and
it turned out it was non-parity RAM.  Needless to say, once I returned it,
I've not gone back there.

I've got a 200 4/233 with 112Mb of RAM, I found that it was sluggish with
only 80Mb of RAM.  Be glad you can use a semi-normal type of memory, I want
to add more RAM to my 500/333, but as near as I can figure out it requires
special memory.

As for the HD's you need the old 50-pin SCSI, unless you put a supported
SCSI controller in the system that will take better drives.  I've personally
not had any problems with 3rd party drives in the 200 4/233, but your milage
may vary.

				Zane

