In article <009D9BA5.810397D2@SSRL04.SLAC.STANFORD.EDU>, 
winston@SSRL.SLAC.STANFORD.EDU ("Alan Winston - SSRL Admin Cmptg Mgr") writes:

> I got kind of carried away and bought an AlphaStation 200 4/233
> and a 4/166 on Ebay.  

Welcome to the club!   I have the same two systems, one for work
and one for hobby use.   Since the 4/233 came without a license,
it became my hobby machine.   Since the 4/166 does an adequate
job for software development, it made sense to keep it that way.

> does anybody have any suggestions for AlphaStation 200 memory sources?

Due to my conservative nature, I went with the Kingston
KTV-TAM/128 (128 MB) kit for my 4/233.   The equivalent DEC P/N
is FR-PC77M-AJ. I paid $365 for it at CDW one year ago.   Their
P/N was 088111.   For my 4/166, I paid $465 for a KTV-TAM/64 in
February 1997.   Isn't it amazing what a little time does to RAM
prices?   I now have 96MB in the 4/166 and 160MB in the 4/233.

> Is it readily known what variant of SCSI (fast, ultra, wide,
> fast wide, plain vanilla, etc) these systems support?  

Narrow fast.   I had some problems installing VMS v7.1 from an
RRD45 CDROM to an RZ26F (mount verification) until I switched it
to slow (pka0_fast = 0), however.

> Can you recommend third party disks that will work in
> these systems?

My 4/166 came with an RZ26F (1 GB) system disk, but I relegated
it to a data disk and installed a Quantum "Atlas" XP31070 (2.1
GB) as my system disk.   I have had no problems with *this*
Quantum disk in over two years.   I chose this drive because
other people had reported good results running VMS from it.

My 4/233 came with no disks.   I decided to use a real DEC drive
for the system disk, so I purchased a DEC refurbished RZ28D-VA
(B2-RZ28D-VA) (2.1 GB) for $150 (July 1998).   It is a Seagate
ST32550N with the DEC microcode.

For my second drive on that system, I chose a Quantum Viking
VK34550S (4.5 GB) from OnSale.com.   This drive gave me fits
until I saw a note on comp.os.vms by Reece Pollack.   He had
worked with Quantum to identify the problem, caused by the "SCAM"
(Self-Configuring Auto-Magically) feature.    This is
incompatible with the SCSI implementation on the AlphaStation
(and several other systems, I might add).   The solution was to
use an MS-DOS utility from Quantum to turn SCAM off by setting
the "DS" (Disable SCAM) bit in Mode Page 39.

The Seagate drive runs quite a bit hotter than the Quantum
drive, but neither has failed yet.

The 4/233 came with only one set of flash ROM chips ("half
flash"), so I purchased  a pair of AM28F020-150JC-ND flash memory
chips from Digi-Key for $27.50.   This allowed me to install
the full ARC/SRM console code, which went very smoothly.

Manuals that you might find useful:

  EK-PCDTA-UI AlphaStation 200 Series User Information
  EK-PCDSA-TI AlphaStation 200/400 Series Technical Information
  EK-T2424-OG ZLXp-E1/E2/E3 PCI Graphics Options Owner's Guide

Our favorite DECies have posted a list of graphics options
that are supported by VMS, so I won't repeat it here.   If you
can't find it, I can forward a copy to you.

Alan

