In article <37615a01.52445923@news.freeserve.net>, 
  k_fluoride@venona.freeserve.co.uk (Klaus Fluoride) writes:

>
>	There are also 4 LEDs, labelled D1 to D4. Upon powering up, D1
>lights and stays on, and D2 flashes every second after 13 seconds of
>power up. None of the others light up.
>
   
	D1 indicates that the unit is receiving power
	D2 blinking means there was a non-fatal hardware error during
	   the self-test, an explanatory message should appear on the
           console terminal - see below. D2 on solid represents a
	   successful self test
        D3 blinks while the unit is attempting to load the software
           image, D3 on solid shows the software has been loaded.
        D4 is "network activity" and will normally blink to show any
           activity on the ethernet. If D4 isn't blinking you have
           a network problem. Of course if you haven't yet connected
           the unit to the ethernet this is normal.

   The red switch is a "reset to factory defaults" button. Holding it
down while you apply power to the unit will initialize everything.
Once you've done this port number 1 should be defined as the console
port. Connect your terminal emulator there, with the speed set to
9600, 8bits, no parity. You should see various self-test messages
appear on the screen. The content of those message will determine
what your next step should be.

   The DECServer 200 has to download its operating software from a
server which runs the MOP protocol ( part of DECNet ), if you haven't set
up that software on a server and connected the DS200 to a network
you aren't going to get very far beyond the steps listed above.

