Klaus Fluoride <k_fluoride@venona.freeserve.co.uk> writes:
> 	That's all. As you can imagine, I'm pretty stumped with it,
> as it should be running a dial-in service for users on a HP3000
> machine on site. I'm screwed without it, so if anyone can offer any
> help or information of any sort whatsoever, please do so!!!

  I think you're screwed *with* it, too. You need a software image for the
box (it only has a bootstrap in it), a host to load from (it uses a DEC
protocol called MOP for booting, though there are MOP server sources around
the net) and it only speaks LAT (a proprietary DEC protocol unlikely to be
found on your HP).

> 	As far as I can assertain, the white switch is a power button
> (which appears to be stuck in or broken) and the red one is a reset.
> Please set me straight on this; like I said, I didn't get a manual or
> anything with the box.

  The white button is the reset for the circuit breaker. The box has no
power switch - if it's plugged in, it's on.

  The red button is a "reset to factory defaults" button - press and hold
while powering up.

> 	There are also 4 LEDs, labelled D1 to D4. Upon powering up, D1
> lights and stays on, and D2 flashes every second after 13 seconds of
> power up. None of the others light up.

  The top one is power Ok.
  The next one is self-test OK if on, testing if off, blinking if there
was a self-test error. In your case, probably because it isn't connected
to an Ethernet.
  The next one is software load - blinking is loading, solid is loaded.
  The bottom one shows network activity.

> 	I've tried interfacing on all the ports to a bog standard
> terminal emulator to go into console mode and see what's what, but I
> can't even get any repsonse from the box no matter what my settings.

  Port 1 is the console port. The default speed is 9600 baud.

> 	Many thanks to anyone who can help...

  If you're looking for TCP/IP support to talk to your host, I'd suggest a
new-style DECserver 700. They speak TCP/IP as well as LAT, and the newer
(actually quite a few years old by now) ones can take a PCMCIA flash card
with the software on it, so they don't have to netboot. Make sure you get
the latest software with any DECserver you buy, or you just have a paper-
weight.

