eBay did a press release on 6/11 (which has, incidentally, been deleted
from the website...apparently Sun paid them off) which said it was
caused by a Sun software problem. eBay also stated that the problem was
NOT caused by excessive traffic. It didn't say what the software was,
but since we know the database is Oracle, I can only assume the
software in question was Solaris.

I continue to be amazed at how few people care about this. eBay lost
five billion dollars in market capitalization due to the problem, and
nobody is blinking an eye, despite the fact that countless other
enterprise installations run Solaris, and who knows how many more
latent bugs there are waiting to case more disasters?  Had this been a
VMS or Microsoft problem, people would be jumping up and down in the
streets. It's absolutely incredible and very, very frightening how
Unix-biased and Unix-forgiving the IT world is.

BTW, there was an interesting article about this "Compaq Hits Sun for
eBay Outage" at http://www.techweb.com/wire/story/TWB19990614S0015
which I haven't seen mentioned here yet. Unfortunately, it mentions
Tru64 and Tandem, but not VMS. ;-(

-- Terry




