Hello,

I just brought my DEC 3000 300LX (125 MHz,  vs 150 MHz 300X models) out of
storage this week.  You are correct in saying the keyboard connection is
weird;  DEC made many proprietary items and interfaces in the 3000 models.
It's currently running, so I cant get a good look at the bottom of the
connector to give you a model number, but will post it in a few hours, so
you can have the option to order or find it.  Basically the keyboard with
the arrow going into the circle is the connector for both the keyboard AND
the mouse;  the cable that comes out of that plug becomes two ports:  one
for a mouse and one for a telephone-like connector for the keyboard.

The serial port is exactly what you'd expect on a PC (at least SOMETHING is
recognizable back there for PC users, heh).  However, you may attach a
switchbox to it, since it also doubles as a parrallell port for a  printer
(preferrably DEC).  Postscript-based printers can be hooked up to it.

Once configured, the following command line works rather well at setting up
the modem configuration as superuser:

cd /usr/sbin
slattach tty00 19200
ifconfig sl0 <IP address> <gateway address> netmask <netmask address>
broadcast <broadcast address, usually 255.255.255.0 I think>
route add default <gateway> 0

It's recommended you put the above into a script. sl0 is your serial line #0
(or 1 in C language).  ln0 is your ethernet port.  I'm a little rusty on DEC
unix but do a few  'man -k <keyword>'  searches and you can find out lots of
good info., if the man pages are set up correctly.   To view the I/O ports
in DEC UNIX, use the 'netstat' command.

Dan

