Jan Mueller wrote:

> Just saved a 3000 300LX AXP Station before it was trashed ... cool box
> for a cool OS.
>

    Speaking of cool, make sure it's in a very cool environment (less than
80�F, or 30�C).  My 3000 300LX crashed & burned once and fried everything
inside except the floppy and hard drive.  They are very sensitive machines. The
hardware manual says it can take 104�F, 40�C conditions, but take this with a
mine of salt.

> On the right side of the board there are 8 LED which begin to flash
> while booting. After some time , these LED's remained in the
> following state:
>
> Back of the box  <- 1 1 1 1 0 1 0 0 ->  Front of the box
>
> Does anybody know this code or is the machine just dead ??

    DEC 3000 model 300LX AXP memory is tricky.  They only allow 8-meg or 32-meg
memory modules, each must be in a pair.  I have the hardware reference manual
here.  Paraphrasing the text, it says "the 8-meg modules (part number MS16-BA)
allow 16-64 megs of memory to be loaded in 16-meg increments...A 32-meg module
(part number MS16-DA) allows 64-256 megs of memory to be added in 64-meg
increments...".   True to DEC proprietary standards, they dont go into detail
what type of memory is required (parity vs. non-parity), but I recall it was
'special ram', so dont just go out and buy 8 or 32-meg 72-pin simms for it, or
they may not work.  If someone can help clarify this, I'd appreciate it, too :)

The error code is read from the front of the box towards the back (thus the
above would really be read 00101111), thus in hexadecimal code giving the
error  4F.  The hardware manual regards this as a "post-powerup LED failure"
(page 8-4) and says it has something to do with the SCC.   All it says after
this is, "Refer to DEC 3000 Model 300 Series AXP Service Guide for a complete
listing of hexadecimal codes, their binary (LED) equivalent, and their
meaning".  The service guide was not included with my machine (sold
separately), thus I cant help much further.  If anyone can clarify what a SCC
is, or the meaning of this error, I'd also appreciate it.

I hate to sound trollish, but a lot of times this computer wasnt worth the time
it took.  Most of the time it was broken, with a burnt-out power supply, ruined
motherboard / chip, powered display not showing anything, or broken keyboards.
Work with this UNIX station at your own risk ;)  It may already be broken, but
hey, the price you got was worth it ;)  at least sell it as parts to a
reseller, or strip the hard drive, turbochannel cards, etc. for parts.

Dan

PS Let me know if it has any Turbochannel cards in it, I'm looking for some ;)
Thanks




