3000/300 machines are a bit tricky when it comes to memory, but not
unusually so.  Your major limitation will be the fact that you can either
use 8 or 32 mb modules as the previous author mentioned.  However, as far as
"special RAM" goes, I've not found that to be the case in the past.  IIRC, a
3000/300 takes 72-pin true-parity (36 bit) SIMMS.  Of particular note might
be that Digital tends to specify JDEC standard, which is merely a grading
standard and specification for SIMMs.  Any good SIMM manufacturer's product
should meet this standard, so as long as you buy reputable, good-quality
memory you should be fine (in other words, don't seek out the rock-bottom
price PeeCee stuff, look for the better grade often specified by the
workstation and PeeCee desktop manufacturers like Compaq or HP).  Kingston
is always a good bet.

