Aargh, I've seen this one before.  They banned smoking in the building where
I used to work, so the systems guys that smoked would head into the computer
room (granted, a glorified closet at the time with enough room for a desk
and few engineering systems servers), and take advantage of the fact that it
had an exhaust fan...

: 
: 2. One thing that can happen (sooner rather that later if your
:    computer room is not kept clean) is that dust can collect on
:    the modules and chips of your equipment, preventing normal
:    heat dissipation, which can lead to crashes, random errors,
:    and all sorts of problems.

Seen this on multiple occasions.  Amazing what a little cleaning will do to
some of the wierder ones I've seen.  The other issue that you'll run into is
with the environmental protection systems on newer equipment.  Most of the
newer AlphaServers I've dealt with have a two or three-stage environmental
monitoring (in the OS AND at the PROM level), which will kick in when heat
becomes an issue.  Since dust buildup can block fans, heat can become an
issue much cleaner.  We were forced to locate a couple of our servers in a
fiber termination room in our corporate facility at my last employer, as it
was the only available area in the only building with a generator backup. 
You'd expect the network/fiber termination room to be very clean, but it
turns out that the telecomms manager had also been using to store old,
outdated equipment that by all rights should have been scrapped.  He
apparently had also volunteered it for storage of the Christmas decorations.

To make a long story short, our database server (one of 2 located in that
room) started going down every day around 3:00.  After a bunch of
troubleshooting and debugging, we finally figured out that when they cleaned
out the room, apparently they'd managed to miss a ton of dust (in a room
poorly ventilated to begin with).  Basically, the environmental monitoring
was doing its job and shutting things down due to overheat conditions.

Needless to say, the HVAC guys were plenty busy plenty quick...

: 
: 3. Maybe you can enlist Compaq's help in getting on your
:    management's case.

If you've got a service agreement with them, its a given they'll get on your
management.  When our Alphas were under Digital service contract, we had a
bunch of machines in a building with what turned out to be sub-standard
wiring.  After a rash of board & monitor replacements, Digital sent a Field
Engineer out to do tests in the building, only to find that our ground was
floating anywhere from a good chunk positive to a decent swing in the
negative direction, and the current wasn't stable at all.  They threatened
to discontinue the service contract on the spot and bill us for all the
warranty work in the past year if it wasn't corrected post-haste. 
Apparently "living conditions" for your hardware is a part of the agreement.

