I believe scanning is only supported on the X86 platform and not very well
at that.

However see the FAQ at http://dutlbcz.lr.tudelft.nl/alphant/

which says

Q. What scanners can be used with an Alpha?
A. Most all SCSI scanners should work with FX!32 v1.4 now. This is one
circumstance where a "driver" (i.e., the TWAIN driver) supplied as 32 bit
x86 code works under FX!32. TWAIN "drivers" are not kernel-mode device
drivers, which FX!32 can not process; they are user-mode components that
simply export a known interface. An application that supports the TWAIN
interface can load TWAIN "drivers" and do image acquires. A TWAIN "driver"
knows how to drive a particular image acquisition device -- it can do this a
number of ways: via ASPI, a kernel-mode driver, invoking another
application, etc. If you have adevice which works on Alpha (via FX!32 or
otherwise) and there is a TWAIN "driver" for that device, you should be able
to make things work. FX!32 v1.4 contains a plugin-provider which should
allow for cross-architecture TWAIN support.

The only hitch is that to date, it's only been able to work when the TWAIN
manager DLL in \WinNT (i.e, TWAIN32.DLL) is x86 code.

This has been tested with both AGFA scanners that use the FotoLook software
and the newer 32 bit HP scanner software that uses WinASPI. In theory, this
should work with digital cameras, too. If you have other successes, let us
know so that this FAQ can be updated!

So you may be able to get something going with FX!32 magic.

See http://pw2.netcom.com/~gmelendz/index.html
for details on getting HP Scanners going with NT, note these instructions
are for X86 only.

Good luck, if you have any success please let me know.

TJW

