Ben Myers wrote:
> 
> My quick and not-really-glib response is that dust and dirt have a lot
> to do with any part of a computer that has exposed moving parts.  So
> computer fans stop spinning, floppy drives and CD-ROM drives don't
> work any more, and tape drives with their tapes surely get mucked up.
> 
> Sounds like there's enough dirt in the computer room to contaminate
> the tapes and/or tape drive heads, both of which ain't cheap for DLTs.
> Then there's the lost time and labor due to down time, reruns, and
> lost data.
> 
> Yes, the computer room is way too dirty.  Computer rooms are meant to
> be kept clean, free of dust and dirt.  That's the way they used to be
> way back when computers were buckets of vacuum tubes.  The world
> hasn't really changed since then.
> 
> One other possible source of unreliable tape drive operation is a bad,
> marginal, or failing power supply.  But if tape drive and tape
> failures are all over the computer room, reject this possibility and
> go for the computer room clean up.  And keep it clean.
> 
> ... Ben Myers
> 
> itjck01@my-deja.com wrote:
> 
> >Hope I have the right newsgroup for this.  If not, then pointing me to
> >the right one would be appreciated.
> 
> >Questions:  If one can see the dirt on the computer room floor, then
> >the room is probably way too dirty?  What is the typical setup of
> >keeping a computer room clean?
> 
> >Problems:
> >Backups are failing due to tape drives calling for cleaning at a
> >preceived high rate.  I am having a hard time politically convincing
> >the operations manager and my management that the computer room needs
> >to be cleaned and kept clean.
> 
> >Detail:
> >I have several Alpha systems with TZ877 tape drives, which are showing
> >a lot of errors on the tape drives from week to week with calls to use
> >cleaning tape, even after Compaq has replaced the tape drives.  (ie: it
> >only takes about a week or so before the tape drives call for a
> >cleaning)  Disk storage (StorageWorks and other) have not been failing,
> >but dust/dirty accumulating on drives, which is monthly removed.  I
> >have not ventured yet to look at the system bus and CPU cabinet yet,
> >but I know I should, and clean very carefully (canned clean compressed
> >air only) the areas.
> 
> >Our tape usage is that we go through about 10 DLT IIIs on one tape
> >drive a week, and seven on another.  The tape media being used is brand
> >new out of the box Compac DLT IIIs.  Environmental temperature and
> >humidity are about 60 degrees F with 40 to 50% humidity.  Air coming in
> >is only superficially filtered (no HEPA filter, just fibreglass filter
> >like home ventilation filters).
> 
> >Thanks in advance for your comments and pieces of computer room
> >operational wisdom.
> 
> >Sent via Deja.com http://www.deja.com/
> >Share what you know. Learn what you don't.


One common cause of problems is poor environmental controls ...

I know one computer room where the conditions are about 65F and 50% 
humidity (on average) ... but the reality is that the air conditioner is
grossly over-sized, so if you put a recorder in there, the temperature
swings from 75 to 50!  And the humidity within 10 minutes goes from 35%
to 60%!  Far too fast for their hygrometer to measure.

They suffered terribly from media and equipment failures, until they put
the equipment in enclosed racks that helps even out some of those
temperature swings.

Stuart



