I am of the opinion that cleanliness is next to godliness --
and perhaps higher.   With that in mind...

Last Friday I had occasion to visit a machine shop for a
client.   To be more precise, this was an EDM (Electrical
Discharge Machining) shop.   EDM is done by machining a
block of graphite electrode into the reverse of what you
eventually want,  then using it (under a bath of dielectric
oil) to erode a hole in (typically) very hard tool steel
using an electrical current.

The process of machining the electrode generates lots of
graphite dust.   The EDM process itself generates a fine
fog of burned oil, combined with microscopic bits of metal.
I can hardly imagine worse substances to have floating
around computer equipment.

When I entered the shop, I noticed that every surface
(including the skin of the owner) was covered with a
black coating.   The PC on his desk was coated in black
dust.   The keyboard was black.   The monitor could
hardly be seen through the black dust.   Yet it worked!!!

I simply cannot understand how this guy's computer can
continue to function in this environment.   Dust is bad
enough, but *conductive* dust?!?!?!   He must live a
charmed life.

