In article <zalmanFCoot1.LyA@netcom.com>
Zalman Stern <zalman@netcom.com> writes:
>(*) There are definitely edge cases in the MIPS compatibility picture that
>probably wouldn't be acceptable in say an x86 type world. E.g. between
>MIPS-I and MIPS-II the one instruction load-delay slot was eliminated and
>loads became interlocked. Technically this is not a violation of backwards
>compatibility because MIPS-I specified that using the result of a load in
>the instruction immediately following the load was undefined.

I don't see how that's at all questionable.

Otherwise, it seems like people may then say that any undefined behavior
is some sort of problem.  I don't think this is desirable.

In the past, there have been problems where architectures aren't clear
on what's undefined and what's not.  It's not good for the answer to
such questions to be, `Well what do the existing machines do?'.

But i think things are better now.  For example, the Alpha spec has
various `UNPREDICTABLE' behavior, but i don't see that as any problem.

If the spec makes clear that some thing, say "push sp", is undefined,
i'd say that's that.  Of course, there must not be security problems;
for example, an opcode that locks up the machine is not good.

--
Joe Keane, amateur mathematician



