I havent been tracking what going on with Digital in some time,
but my VAXstation is getting long in the tooth, and I am hearing
rumors of some awesome systems coming down the pike.  Id like to
get a Alpha box which I could use for development for OpenVMS and
Digital UNIX (er, Tru64 UNIX), and if it ran NT and Linux for Alpha,
all the better.

<Venting>
The problem is trying to find out what machines I should be looking
at.  The web sites are a trip.  The Digital workstation web site only
talks about the 500a, 500au, 600a, 600au and the 533 SMP.  The Compaq
workstation website only talks about the XP1000.  If you click on
the graphics options on the left side of the picture of an 'au' box
you get a set of Compaq adapters, seemingly none of which works with
these boxes, and if you click on the right side you get what appears
to be a subset of the available 3Dxx and 4Dxx cards.  If you download
the price list Windows application, you get a different smattering of
devices along with something called a VS10, which a search from the
page http://www.digital.com provides no matches on (thanks to a poster
in this group I found a press release on the VS10.)  Talking to
Digital hasnt been much better, when it took me a full month to get an
upgrade price for VMS on my VAXstation ($10,000 for a workstation with
an orignal list price of around $6,000 with a high resolution color
monitor, and a new VMS license?  Its cheaper to buy a whole new machine
each time!!!)
</Venting>

The 'au' boxes are obsolete technology, but they seem to be fine
candidates for VMS and UNIX development.  Stepping outside the
workstation line leads to the DS10, which might be an excellent
option if:

  1: I can get single user licenses at workstation prices, particularly
     the Digital UNIX license addition to a VMS box and C++ compilers
     (an affordable DECnet Phase IV for both would be a nice bonus!)
  2: I can change operating systems through alternate partitioning
     or some kind of carrier based disk setup - preferably SCSI based.
     Reloading firmware before each switch is just out of the question.
  3: I can get some kind of 24-bit graphics card for the thing (32MB
     video cards capable of low end 3D at 1920x1200x32@76Hz as well as
     DVD decoding and TV out are under $200 for PCs..  Its time to let
     go of 8-bit only display cards!!!)

Back to the workstation line, the VS10 looks like it could be an
excellent multi-OS development platform if it supported multiple OSs,
which it does not appear to.  The XP1000 is an over the top box, but
there appear to be no plans to support VMS on it.

I would appreciate any suggestions on what would be a good solution
here for developing for the Alpha.  I would also like to hear when
these boxes will support the latest and greatest, which appear to
be OpenVMS 7.2 and Tru64 UNIX 5.0, both of which are referenced in
Digitals web pages but neither of which seems to be available for
the workstations (or anything for the UNIX) as of yet.  Thanks!

-Steve



