Peter da Silva (peter@baileynm.com) wrote:
: Would you say the PDP-11 was or was not one architecture?

I don't know very much about the PDP-11. But my claim is that POWER and
PowerPC have been two slightly different lines of development while each
successive version of the MIPS architecture should run any valid program
the previous version ran. There are MIPS implementations that have
extensions (e.g. integer multiply accumulate, for which there are two or
three incompatible versions), but those are outside the base architecture
spec. (And all conforming MIPS implementations must provide everything in
the base spec.  See the Lexera case where they were not allowed to call
their chip a MIPS implementation because they left out the unaligned load
instructions.)

: >Kind of sad given that IBM pretty much defined the standard of
: >architectural stability with the 360 and successors...

: By making extensive use of emulation.

Which is fine, but at least there is a single architectural entity that
defines the instruction set for software. And of course there are
aberations, such as the vector facility.

To put it another way, IBM may implement PowerPC workstations using POWER
chips and software emulation, but that does not mean POWER and PowerPC are
compatible. This gets back to architecture vs implementation. (Depending on
context, PDP-11 could denote either a machine or the architecture.)

I gather POWER3 is a superset of POWER2 and PowerPC (sans AltiVec of
course). Which is cool, but its pretty late in the game for it to matter.

-Z-



