Well, I tried my newly minted OpenVMS hobbyist CD-ROM in my machine in
hopes to finally getting my Vaxstation 3100 M38 off the ground. I have
previously installed OpenVMS 6.1 with the old hobbyist CD-ROM, but with
no ANSI terminal, and no support for the graphics console, it severely
limited my options. The Montagar Software Concepts Hobbyist OpenVMS Vax
kit has lots of potential for opening my options.

So, I got me a new Seagate 1.06GB drive which is freshly formatted via
the "TEST 75" command, tried to do the install last night. I tried for
two hours to no avail. Here's as much information as I could think to
provide in hopes that someone out there has insight into the problem.

Configuration:  Vaxstation 3100 M38, GPX color console with VR299
monitor
                        Compaq 2X-CDROM by Toshiba, model CR-503, set to
512 byte sectors, device 1 on SCSI-A (DKA100:)
                        Seagate ST31230N 1.06 GB hard drive, Device 2 on
SCSI-A (DKA200:)
                        IBM "Options" series 364MB hard drive, Device 3
on SCSI-A(DKA300:) - OVMS version 6.1 boot disk
-----------------------------------------------
Upon powerup, the powerup sequence is:


KA42B     V1.5

F...E...D...C...B...A...9...8...7?..6...5...4...3...2...1...

??7 80A0    0E06.31D0
? 6  80A1    0000.4001

>>> SHOW DEV
VMS/VMB    ULTRIX    ADDR                                NUMBYTES
DEVNAM            REV
ESA0            SE0            08-00-2B-14-98-1F

DKA100        RZ1            A/1/0/0            RODISK    681
MB           CD-ROM            1.1C
DKA200                           A/2/0/0            DISK
1.05    GB           SEAGATE           0532
DKA300                           A/3/0/0            DISK
364     MB           DSAS-33547      Q

>>> B DKA100:
------------------------------------------------

At this point the standalone backup boots from the CR-DOM just dandy. I
type in the new system time, just like page 4-2 of the new install
manual says, and the system then configures the devices. I then enter
this command, just like it is printed in the manual:

$ BACKUP/IMAGE DKA100:VMS072.B/SAVE DKA200:

Instead of the system launching into the copy of the image onto the
destination disk like what I expected, my install prematurely ends with
system hanging after it prints this error message:

%SYSTEM-I-MOUNTVER, SABKUP$DKA100: is offline. Mount Verification in
progress.

-----------------------------------------------------
At this time, the system quits. No more dollar signs, no system halts,
no chevrons, no nothing.

I am effectively stopped. I need help! To whom shallI turn?

Daniel Burk, Haslett Michigan




