In article <7j7eqh$t8a$1@hiline.shinbiro.com>, "Kyoung-Hwa Lee" <kyonghwa@shinbiro.com> writes:
|> 
|> I faild to do 'su -' like this :
|> 
|> $ su -
|> You do not have permission to su root
|> 
|> Is there any configuration file with su?
|> If that, Let me know it.
|> 

From the su(1) man page:

"Only users who belong to group number 0 (system) can issue su to
 become root, even with the root password."

Group membership is called out in /etc/group .  On a UNIX system with
security restrictions in place, the relevant information _may_ be
stored elsewhere.

$ man 1 su
$ man 4 group

Regards,
=Aaron
