tjb@Starbase.NeoSoft.COM (Timothy J. Bogart) writes:

>In article <7j1g6o$17n$1@murrow.corp.sgi.com>,
>John McCalpin <mccalpin@engr.sgi.com> wrote:

>>SPEC only measures compile time for one particular compiler,
>>gcc, cross-compiling a particular code for a particular target.

>Most _vendors_ don't.  Most vendors don't make compilers
>that can be tested cross platform.  However, lots of folks
>do use GCC.  But, I think the crux is that SPEC is an
>average (ok, geometric mean) of several measures.  Does this
>correlate well with compile time?  Well,  I looked at 4
>examples:  Alpha server 8400, Dell Precision 610, HP9000/785
>and IBM H50.  The ratio of peak SPEC ratio for the .gcc testt
>to the SPECint number advertised are... .85 .89 .80 .91 - 
>that qualifies as a range, but is that what you had in mind
>when you you said "*much*"?


I believe John was referring to the time taken by the native compiler,
NOT gcc.  And please note that the Intel results do not use gcc, and
that the SPEC results I've seen using gcc have been about 30% slower
than the official Intel results on the exact same hardware.  And the x86
is probably gcc's best platform (other than old Sparc CPUs and VAX
perhaps)  gcc is a good compiler if you want a universal compiler that
will run on many systems, and is also good if you like fast compilation.
It is not a good choice if you seek performance.  In a world where people
will pay twice as much for a machine that is 20% faster, I find it amazing
how many of those people will use gcc for their builds.  OK, maybe for
development, since it is faster -- but not for production code when a
better alternative is available!

Some of the slowness that a compiler exhibits may be due to its working
harder to find better optimizations.  That is not always the case, of
course, and your return in execution speed on longer compilation times
reach a point of diminishing returns, as do all things, but there is
sometimes something to be said for letting a compiler chew on your code
for a few hours for your production version.

-- 
