Ok, umm.  Is the thought that the hardware would be better 
categorized by (if they even existed) using more brands of 
compilers?  Or perhaps some comparison to the native compiler?
I have the same compiler running (the same) 50 (cross) compiles 
to compare compile speeds on different hardware. Seems to me
the only better comparison would be to use your chosen brand
of compiler on 50 sets of source code on the machines in 
question.  But so far it sounds like a good approach, no?

>Most vendors do not use gcc as their primary compiler, and 
>the compilation times for the SPECint95 codes probably vary
>*much* more widely than the SPECint95 results.  (At least 
>they have every time I have looked into this in the past.)

Most _vendors_ don't.  Most vendors don't make compilers
that can be tested cross platform.  However, lots of folks
do use GCC.  But, I think the crux is that SPEC is an
average (ok, geometric mean) of several measures.  Does this
correlate well with compile time?  Well,  I looked at 4
examples:  Alpha server 8400, Dell Precision 610, HP9000/785
and IBM H50.  The ratio of peak SPEC ratio for the .gcc testt
to the SPECint number advertised are... .85 .89 .80 .91 - 
that qualifies as a range, but is that what you had in mind
when you you said "*much*"?

To put it another way, looking at the SPECint results, I would
expect the Alpha to be 1) 25% faster than the Dell   2) 17%
faster than the HP  3) 110% faster than the IBM.

The corresponding comparisons for just the .gcc portions:
1) 18%  2) 23% 3) 96%

Is this the sort of variance you object to?
