In article <7j1lej$i6q$1@Starbase.NeoSoft.COM>,
Timothy J. Bogart <tjb@Starbase.NeoSoft.COM> wrote:
>
>But, I think the crux is that SPEC is an
>average (ok, geometric mean) of several measures.  Does this
>correlate well with compile time?  Well,  I looked at 4
>examples:  Alpha server 8400, Dell Precision 610, HP9000/785
>and IBM H50.  The ratio of peak SPEC ratio for the .gcc testt
>to the SPECint number advertised are... .85 .89 .80 .91 - 
>that qualifies as a range, but is that what you had in mind
>when you you said "*much*"?

This is not what I was talking about at all....

My comment was a reminder that the fact that SPECint95
scores are similar does not imply that the time required
to compile the SPECint95 suite was similar on the various
platforms.  

Suppose we took, for example, the following set of 
systems:

---------------------------------------------------------
System				CPU		SPECint95
---------------------------------------------------------
Dell Precision 410		Xeon/450	  18.9
Digital AlphaServer 4100	21164/600	  18.8
HP J2240			PA-8200/236	  17.4
SGI Origin2000			R12000/300	  18.1
Sun Enterprise 450		UltraSparc2/400	  17.4
---------------------------------------------------------

Even though the SPECint95 scores vary by only few percent,
my conjecture is that the time required to compile the 
SPEC benchmarks (using the vendor's native compiler, the 
vendor's filesystem, the vendor's choice of disk drives, etc)
probably differs by a factor of 2 or more.

This information is not reported as part of a SPEC submission,
but it probably correlates better with the "feel" of the
speed of the system than the SPECint95 score, which would
rate these systems as being indistinguishable.
-- 
