I worked at IBM Austin on the GigaProcessor (AKA Power 4) and worked closely with
the fellows
who developed the 630FP (AKA Power 3) and they felt that is was very much closer
to the
Power architecture (Power, Power 2, P2SC =Power 2 Single Chip) than the IBM +
Motorola
PowerPC - That blurb you just quoted is Marketing the Architects and the
Engineers who worked
on it. It just so happens that IBM, Motorola, And Apple have already spent a lot
of money on PowerPC
marketing and there hasn't been much on the IBM only Power architecture. Kind of
like Intel's Pentium
trade mark - care to guess the Marketing name for IA-64.

The PowerPC architecture is specified at 3 levels - 1) User Instruction Set
Architecture

2) Virtual Environment Architecture

3) Operating Environment Architecture

All of the processors are more or less compatible at the User Instruction Set
Architecture with some
incompatibilities of a minor nature.

The other 2 levels have various degrees of compatibility between implementations.
The PowerPC name
is wider known but the Power name carries the promise of more performance so
maybe IBM wants it
both ways.

Anybody else who is in the know care to comment. What is the philosophy of Power
versus PowerPC
is that distinction can still be made?

You might like to know that IBM is combing the AS400 PowerAS (PowerPC derivitive)
and the
PowerPC architecture in a move to consolidate the processor architectures of its
major groups.

All opinions are my own.

Chris Gomez
Millennium Architecture Performance Analysis and Simulation (aka UltraSparc 5)
Sun Microelectronics
cagomez@eng.sun.com





