I worked at IBM Austin on the GigaProcessor (AKA Power 4) .

The PowerPC architecture is specified at 3 levels -

1) User Instruction Set Architecture

2) Virtual Environment Architecture

3) Operating Environment Architecture

All of the processors are more or less compatible at the User Instruction Set
Architecture with some incompatibilities of a minor nature.

The other 2 levels have various degrees of compatibility between implementations.

The PowerPC name is wider known but the Power name carries the promise of more
performance so
maybe IBM wants it both ways.

Anybody else who is in the know care to comment. What is the philosophy of Power
versus PowerPC
if that distinction can still be made?

If PowerPC is the "only" architecture left what is to become of the Power
instructions (Power1, Power2,
P2SC- Power3 if any).

I was under the impression that the two architectures had diverged and then they
would converge
after the Somerset breakup - IBM wants to take PowerPC (with some Power goodies
and some
AS400 additions => Power 4 or GigaProcessor) into the high end and Motorola wants
to keep it on
the desktop (ala Apple). Both want to take at least the User Instruction Set
Architecture into the
Embedded world.

You might like to know that IBM is combing the AS400 PowerAS (PowerPC derivitive)
and the
PowerPC architecture in a move to consolidate the processor architectures of its
major groups.

All opinions are my own.

