Chris Gomez (cagomez@eng.sun.com) wrote:
: All of the processors are more or less compatible at the User Instruction Set
: Architecture with some
: incompatibilities of a minor nature.

Some of us just cut to the chase and translate "more or less," when applied
to architectural compatibility, to "not compatible." In the case of
POWER/PowerPC/POWER2 one can specify a subset of all three that is common
and that could suffice to compile user programs. (It wouldn't be the only
architecture without an integer multiply instruction :-)) One can also have
the OS emulate instructions that are not present on the processor one is
currently running on. (Turning a compatibility problem into a possible
performance issue.)

But there is a fundamental difference between MIPS, which is a series of
architectures, each of which is a complete superset(*) of the previous
version, and [Pp][Oo][Ww][Ee][Rr]([23]|(PC))? (hairy regexp showing that
they can't even really agree on a naming convention) in which there are two
main lines kind of going in slightly different directions with periodic
attempts at mergers (601, POWER3). This meandering wrongheadedness
continues with IBM's decision to basically ignore AltiVec. To a software
developer, this is a very bad thing. Though its largely irrelevant as any
promise of commonality in hardware and software environments across various
Power-something-or-other platforms proved to be hype years ago.

Kind of sad given that IBM pretty much defined the standard of
architectural stability with the 360 and successors...

(*) There are definitely edge cases in the MIPS compatibility picture that
probably wouldn't be acceptable in say an x86 type world. E.g. between
MIPS-I and MIPS-II the one instruction load-delay slot was eliminated and
loads became interlocked. Technically this is not a violation of backwards
compatibility because MIPS-I specified that using the result of a load in
the instruction immediately following the load was undefined. Another
example is the fungible definition of floating-point accumulate, which can
be fused or unfused depending on the MIPS implementations. I consider that
one a sizeable mistake architecturally, but that largely depends on how
SGI's customers feel about small amounts of slop in the output from the
same binary on different machines. And SGI knows the answer to that far
better than I do.

-Z-



